aggard, and Ed[ward] Blount, published 1623, →OCLC, [Act IV, scene iii]:"}, {"text": "He [Edward the Confessor] was the first that touched for the evil.", "reference": "1711 March 23 (Gregorian calendar), [Joseph Addison; Richard Steele et al.], “MONDAY, March 13, 1710–1711”, in The Spectator, number 329; republished in Alexander Chalmers, editor, The Spectator; a New Edition, […], volume IV, New York, N.Y.: D[aniel] Appleton & Company, 1853, →OCLC:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": ["good"]}, {"language": {"code": "en", "name": "English"}, "partOfSpeech": "adverb", "pronunciations": [{"type": "ipa", "text": "/ˈiːvəl/", "tags": []}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "[ˈɪi̯vəl] ~ [ˈɪi̯vl̩]", "tags": []}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "/ˈiːvɪl/", "tags": []}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "[ˈɪi̯vɪl]", "tags": ["obsolete"]}], "forms": [{"word": "more evil", "tags": ["comparative"]}, {"word": "most evil", "tags": ["superlative"]}], "senses": [{"definition": "(obsolete) wickedly, evilly, iniquitously", "tags": ["obsolete"], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "O what of Gods then boots it to be borne, / If old Aveugles ſonnes ſo euill heare?", "reference": "1596, Edmund Spenser, “Book I”, in The Faerie Queene. […], part I (books I–III), London: […] [Richard Field] for William Ponsonbie, →OCLC, stanza ii, page 66:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(obsolete) injuriously, harmfully; in a damaging way.", "tags": ["obsolete"], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "And many ſhall follow their pernicious wayes, by reaſon of whom the way of trueth ſhall be euill ſpoken of:", "reference": "1611, The Holy Bible, […] (King James Version), London: […] Robert Barker, […], →OCLC, 2 Peter 2:2:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(obsolete) badly, poorly; in an insufficient way.", "tags": ["obsolete"], "examples": ["It went evil with him."], "quotes": [{"text": "But (as the Poet ſaith) Malè ſarta gratia, nequicquam coit, & reſcinditur: Friendſhip, that is but euill peeced, will not ioine cloſe, but falleth aſunder againe:", "reference": "1570, William Lambard, quoting Horace, A Perambulation of Kent, published 1596, page 341:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": []}], "source": {"url": "https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/evil", "license": {"name": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/"}}}}